<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    No plaintext HTTP, anywhere.

    It used to be permitted globally, which mattered more here than in most
    apps. Whisper fetches whatever addresses its subscriptions name, renders
    the result in a WebView with JavaScript on, and signs in to self-hosted
    Google Reader servers with a username and a password. Over HTTP those
    credentials cross the network in the clear and anybody on the path can
    rewrite the article body before it reaches the reader.

    The cost is that an http-only feed now fails to fetch. That failure is
    visible rather than silent — three failed syncs and the source is listed
    under Broken feeds with its reason — which is a better outcome than
    quietly accepting a page anyone on the wifi could have written.

    The removed block also carried a <domain> of "file:///android_asset/",
    which is not a domain and matched nothing.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
